323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171321 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171321 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171321 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ddc9ab7-6d81-5c34-adce-3d51172ec885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171322 #edb/ref 1080 "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171322 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171322 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d219f0f-39c4-5862-af5d-12791bde950c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171323 #edb/ref 1080 "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171323 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171323 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b45cb8f7-107e-57d7-bcce-5f7edddf8eba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171324 #edb/ref 1080 "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171324 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171324 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4450e4a-a83d-5d96-a4c2-72571169f71f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171325 #edb/ref 1080 "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171325 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171325 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31964a19-89f9-5567-b61a-aeb0b9c5d51e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171326 #edb/ref 1080 "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171326 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171326 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56a62c21-c273-5af3-b6a7-6447609edb48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171831 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171831 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c3/c3a4052f80fc4a7202d410f3ea523e58507ac60399506c2abad9be164bce5251.png)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/18/18abb9c5165a3fe54a0f3067cf5325bfd4bd0fa4243c841ce4e7bb95c25dddda.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a7b5d23-3dc7-57f8-98ed-72b1a45e787a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171831 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171832 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171832 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c3/c344041bbcf1cc644f0cfb1e5a3ac8ecd687db483425e4f1f4bb19c76ebac439.png)\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D=P^T \\! A P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d2dc7ea-947d-5cda-8c95-c28281ecd792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171832 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=5\\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/7b/7b0367066950407149521e6b06255f71b25fe5e67bc609fac5e914d3d1315fe7.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/49/498ecfed1cfe5ed4b1df8c02088045e67c4d5601e47f403288d271447c5ded28.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/0a/0aa3aecabd941ccc8e176aef479eb3630197ff9c7fb6ca63e028e41315c9aafb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](images/1f/1f494272dcd6828d437e2f566e46aa5b5ece49a92cd82af094e25834fe1bbcbe.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/73/737d19f3df39ef56518c98843cb7dd0359965570892b17df57fb4117bb0f5844.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171833 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171833 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/96/9614ff63a66cf5428a4107828b08ba16c43e3e021f6620d408d8b70358a3e535.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/9a/9acec021ce321e77ad541ecbb0a38944aa4991ec26c721e0e59a521490269d06.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd780d94-dd75-5a57-b34d-4bbaffcb95ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171833 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/ef/efb691aec933aa631974add9dc9d7d122b03f5956b17459eb0e76751e07ea42a.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/8c/8c0ac2201e3e40bcc8e5e71c12413504694b201237069a4695fae94d33e7fcb4.png)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/14/1490159efc1ac2073ee029f918c136b7c16fd77b5f15e59334a3ae63e08a8c92.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172025 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" #edb/ref 1759