times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ceb5619f-2165-5346-b048-85008466cdea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272153 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272154 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6cb0f9e-f73e-53e5-b7a2-f0d1aeb7047d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272154 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272156 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d04c9e9d-40ba-57d1-8646-0a16510ea6a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272156 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354694 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354694 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354694 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04f5183c-a1e7-52fc-a81b-7d2fba7a799b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354971 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354971 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354971 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24b3a306-6222-5905-a512-587f49756a4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355134 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355134 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355134 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "34f12e7e-48a1-568f-b505-e3ad0aa65f36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355827 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355827 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355827 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "840e5a6c-9203-57f7-bd34-b3557aaceaa6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355832 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355832 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355832 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "846cae1c-da01-5d91-ad29-413f58d07fde" #edb/ref 1759218