c matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357621 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186357350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357621 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357621 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357621 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{13} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{5}{13} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357621 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a90b3ef-f0f0-5198-bb72-8d712e554612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357621 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364509 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "987ecebc-d297-5962-b8d0-90f351ffca4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364509 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-10)I & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{aligned}]+10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-10)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364510 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "537c6748-dc18-5328-8608-350f3c43bf42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364511 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364511 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364511 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56f0e8db-19ca-5e1a-acef-433c78628572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364512 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364512 #edb/r