074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364517 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7bd4411f-b5f0-53e2-9b19-b5941f836d1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364518 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364518 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364518 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56bc7cf9-dd1b-5316-add9-231be19d109f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364519 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364519 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364519 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b27a1670-2d5b-5bba-92a6-f0a17c0ce246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364520 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "881bcb91-1f0d-5fd7-97a5-86876b601912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364521 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364521 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364521 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d84e1a54-ac78-5b2a-a104-f35ae3afdc41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364522 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db75b88c-c492-572e-968f-86146a9098af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394894 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394894 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394894 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394894 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394894 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5923242-1a47-5487-a3a8-4c37dcb336a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394894 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ Computing $A-(-9)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+9I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394895 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7113dc5c-6527-51ab-8e9c-b39f83dfb5dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394896 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4661f467-2570-50d1-8ea7-764925bd6483" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
